/** @mainpage %Qore dataframe Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section dataframeintro dataframe Module Introduction

    The \c dataframe module provides a columnar DataFrame data structure for the
    <a href="../../lang/html/index.html">Qore programming language</a>, enabling
    typed, SQL-like data manipulation for enterprise data science workloads.

    Features:
    - Typed columns: \c float64 (Eigen3-backed), \c int64, \c string, \c bool, \c date
    - Null/missing value support with per-column null masks
    - SQL-like operations: select, filter, sort, groupby, join, pivot, melt, window functions
    - Dense column interop with \c buffer<T> and @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult"
      for SQL and pipeline workloads
    - Native plugin-operator support: \c frame["column"] for columns,
      \c frame[row] for row hashes, \c frame[start..stop] for row slices, and
      \c frame[mask] for predicate masks
    - I/O: CSV (streaming reader), Parquet (optional Apache Arrow), database (Qore DBI)
    - ML integration: toMatrix/toVector/fromMatrix for zero-overhead ML feeding
    - Thread-safe with cooperative cancellation support

    To use this module, use \c "%requires dataframe" in your code.

    @section dataframeapi dataframe Module API

    Main classes:
    - @ref DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame" - the columnar data structure
    - @ref DataFrame::ColumnRef "ColumnRef" - lazy column reference for predicate masks
    - @ref DataFrame::RowMask "RowMask" - bitmap row selector returned by column comparisons
    - @ref DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame "GroupedDataFrame" - grouped DataFrame for aggregation

    Result hashdecls:
    - @ref DataFrame::DataFrameShape "DataFrameShape" - returned by
      @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::shape() "DataFrame::shape()"
    - @ref DataFrame::ColumnStats "ColumnStats" - returned by
      @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::describe() "DataFrame::describe()"
    - @ref DataFrame::CsvOptions "CsvOptions" - accepted by
      @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::readCSV() "DataFrame::readCSV()" and
      @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::writeCSV() "DataFrame::writeCSV()"

    @section dataframe_typed_examples Typed DataFrame Examples

    DataFrame methods that produce another frame now declare
    @ref DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame" directly, and grouped operations
    declare @ref DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame "GroupedDataFrame" or
    @ref DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame" according to the value returned.
    Methods that return fixed record shapes use typed hashdecls:

    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe

DataFrame::DataFrame employees(
    ({"dept": "sales", "name": "Alice", "salary": 65000.0},
     {"dept": "sales", "name": "Bob", "salary": 70000.0},
     {"dept": "eng", "name": "Carol", "salary": 95000.0}));

hash<DataFrame::DataFrameShape> shape = employees.shape();
printf("rows=%d cols=%d\n", shape.rows, shape.cols);

DataFrame::DataFrame sales = employees.filter("dept", "==", "sales");
DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame grouped = employees.groupBy(("dept",));
DataFrame::DataFrame means = grouped.mean();

list<hash<DataFrame::ColumnStats>> stats = employees.describe();
foreach hash<DataFrame::ColumnStats> stat in (stats) {
    printf("%s: %s count=%d\n", stat.name, stat.dtype, stat.count);
}
    @endcode

    DataFrame also registers native plugin-operator operations for common
    access patterns. Row slices use normal inclusive Qore range semantics,
    including open-ended forms:

    @code{.py}
list<auto> names = employees["name"];
hash<auto> second_row = employees[1];
DataFrame::DataFrame first_two = employees[0..1];
DataFrame::DataFrame reversed = employees[2..0];
DataFrame::DataFrame from_second = employees[1..];
    @endcode

    Predicate masks use explicit column references so direct column access stays
    simple and predictable. Compare a @ref DataFrame::ColumnRef "ColumnRef" with
    a scalar to get a @ref DataFrame::RowMask "RowMask"; then pass the mask to
    \c frame[mask] or @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::filter(DataFrame::RowMask) "filter(mask)".
    The comparison operators are registered plugin operations and work in AST,
    IR, and JIT execution:

    @code{.py}
DataFrame::ColumnRef salary = employees.column("salary");
DataFrame::RowMask high_salary = salary >= 90000.0;
DataFrame::DataFrame senior = employees[high_salary];

DataFrame::DataFrame sales = employees.filter(employees.column("dept") == "sales");
DataFrame::DataFrame known_salary = employees[employees.column("salary") != NOTHING];

printf("selected %d of %d rows\n", high_salary.count(), high_salary.size());
    @endcode

    Supported predicate operators are \c ==, \c !=, \c <, \c <=, \c >, and
    \c >= against scalar \c int, \c float, \c number, \c string, \c bool,
    \c date, \c null, and \c NOTHING values. \c NOTHING equality selects null
    rows; \c NOTHING inequality selects non-null rows. Ordered comparisons with
    \c NOTHING do not select rows.

    @section dataframe_dense_columnar Dense Buffers And Columnar Results

    DataFrames can be constructed from dense \c buffer<T> values for primitive
    numeric and boolean columns. Buffer-backed columns keep a reference to the
    original dense storage, so \c getColumnBuffer(), \c toColumnarResult(), and
    \c fromColumnarResult() can move primitive columns between DataFrame,
    @ref buffer "buffer<T>", and
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult" without first boxing every
    cell into a list. String and date columns currently remain list-backed.

    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe

DataFrame::DataFrame scores({
    "id": new buffer<int64>((1001, 1002, 1003, 1004)),
    "score": new buffer<float64>((95.5, 88.0, 92.0, 81.5)),
    "active": new buffer<bool>((True, False, True, True)),
    "name": ("Alice", "Bob", "Carol", "Dave"),
});

buffer<int64> ids = scores.getColumnBuffer("id");
ColumnarResult columns = scores.toColumnarResult();
buffer<float64> dense_scores = columns.getColumn("score");
list<string> names = columns.getColumn("name");

buffer<bool> high_score = dense_scores >= 90.0;
ColumnarResult selected_columns = columns.filter(high_score);
DataFrame::DataFrame selected = DataFrame::DataFrame::fromColumnarResult(selected_columns);
    @endcode

    ColumnarResult masks can be composed in native code with
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::combineMasks() "combineMasks()" and
    @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult::invertMask() "invertMask()". This is useful
    when code receives typed columns dynamically and cannot rely on parse-time
    vectorized operator resolution for a column variable:

    @code{.py}
buffer<bool> high = columns.getColumn("score") >= 90.0;
buffer<bool> active = columns.getColumn("active") == True;
buffer<bool> selected_mask = columns.combineMasks(high, active, "&&");
ColumnarResult high_active = columns.filter(selected_mask);
    @endcode

    Use @ref DataFrame::DataFrame::concat() "DataFrame::concat()" with
    \c axis:1 for horizontal concat. This shares existing column data when
    possible and rejects mismatched row counts or duplicate output column names:

    @code{.py}
DataFrame::DataFrame left({"id": new buffer<int64>((1, 2, 3))});
DataFrame::DataFrame right({"score": new buffer<float64>((90.0, 80.0, 70.0))});
DataFrame::DataFrame combined = DataFrame::DataFrame::concat((left, right), axis: 1);
    @endcode

    DataFrame kernels still maintain the module's existing internal vectors for
    compatibility with groupby, joins, window functions, and ML conversion.
    Therefore source buffers are preserved for columnar export and dense column
    access, while operations that filter, reorder, aggregate, or reshape rows
    may allocate new column storage.

    @section dataframe_dataprovider_pipelines DataProvider Pipeline Integration

    The companion \c DataProviderDataFrame user module wraps a DataFrame as a
    DataProvider and lets DataFrames flow through
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderPipeline "DataProviderPipeline" in
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderShape::DataFrameBlock "DataFrameBlock" shape.
    DataFrames can also be submitted as ColumnarResult values, which the
    pipeline treats as
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderShape::BufferColumns "BufferColumns" shape.
    DataFrame-aware processors can keep DataFrameBlock representation,
    dense-column processors can keep BufferColumns representation, and other
    bulk-aware processors receive a hash-of-lists or row conversion.

    @code{.py}
%requires DataProviderDataFrame

hash<DataProviderExpression> expr = <DataProviderExpression>{
    "exp": ">=",
    "args": (<DataProviderFieldReference>{"field": "salary"}, 90000.0),
};

list<hash<auto>> selected;
DataProviderPipeline pipeline(<PipelineOptionInfo>{
    "name": "senior-compensation",
    "output_callback": sub (auto row) {
        push selected, row;
    },
});

pipeline.append(new QoreFilterRecordsProcessor({"where_cond": expr}));
pipeline.submit(employees);
pipeline.waitDone();
    @endcode

    QoreFilterRecordsProcessor compiles simple DPQL/DataProvider scalar
    comparisons to DataFrame row masks or dense ColumnarResult boolean masks
    when possible. Supported compiled forms include \c &&, \c ||, \c !, and
    scalar \c ==, \c !=, \c <, \c <=, \c >, and \c >= comparisons. Predicates
    that use string operators, field-to-field comparisons, null literal
    handling, or unsupported column containers fall back to the generic row
    semantics to preserve behavior.

    @code{.py}
ColumnarResult dense_columns = employees.toColumnarResult();
hash<DataProviderExpression> high_value = DataProvider::parseDpqlExpression(
    "@salary >= 90000.0 && @active == true");

DataProviderPipeline dense_pipeline(<PipelineOptionInfo>{
    "name": "dense-filter",
    "output_callback": sub (auto row) {
        printf("%y\n", row);
    },
});
dense_pipeline.append(new QoreFilterRecordsProcessor({"where_cond": high_value}));
dense_pipeline.submit(dense_columns);
dense_pipeline.waitDone();
    @endcode

    CSV option hashes can also be typed explicitly:

    @code{.py}
hash<DataFrame::CsvOptions> options = {
    "separator": "\t",
    "header": True,
    "null_string": "NULL",
};

DataFrame::DataFrame tsv = DataFrame::DataFrame::readCSV("employees.tsv", options);
tsv.writeCSV("employees-copy.tsv", options);
    @endcode

    A DataFrame itself is intentionally not generic. Operations such as
    select(), groupBy(), join(), pivot(), and melt() can change the row shape
    at runtime based on selected columns or remote data. Use typed hashdecls
    for stable result records and \c hash<auto> for dynamic rows.

    @section dataframerelease dataframe Module Release History

    @subsection dataframe_1_0 dataframe Module Version 1.0
    - initial public release with full SQL-like query operations, CSV/Parquet/database I/O,
      and ML integration
    - native plugin-operator access with column, row, and inclusive row-range
      subscripts, plus ColumnRef comparison predicates returning RowMask
      objects accepted by \c df[mask] and \c df.filter(mask)
    - dense \c buffer<T> column interop through DataFrame construction,
      \c getColumnBuffer(), \c toColumnarResult(), and
      \c fromColumnarResult(), preserving buffer-backed primitive columns under
      Qore copy-on-write semantics where possible
    - ColumnarResult filtering with dense boolean masks and horizontal
      DataFrame concat with \c DataFrame::concat(axis:1)
    - integration with DataProvider pipelines through the \c DataProviderDataFrame
      module, including DataFrame bulk reads, DataFrameBlock propagation,
      BufferColumns propagation for ColumnarResult values, and vectorized
      DPQL/DataProvider filter masks for supported comparisons
    - API metadata and generated Doxygen signatures identify DataFrame-returning
      operations as @ref DataFrame::DataFrame "DataFrame", grouped operations as
      @ref DataFrame::GroupedDataFrame "GroupedDataFrame", and fixed result
      hashes as @ref DataFrame::DataFrameShape "DataFrameShape",
      @ref DataFrame::ColumnStats "ColumnStats", and
      @ref DataFrame::CsvOptions "CsvOptions"
*/
